    description:
      "Among 268 students in a ten-week school improv program, those who screened positive for social phobia reported lower social anxiety afterward, with gains in social skills, hope and willingness to make mistakes; two randomized experiments (n = 74 and 131) found 20 minutes of improv exercises increased uncertainty tolerance, divergent thinking and positive affect relative to matched social interaction. A pilot of improv-based group therapy reduced anxiety and depression in 32 psychiatric outpatients, a crossover study found a Second City program feasible and enjoyable for people with Parkinson's disease, a case series describes comedic improv therapy for social anxiety disorder, and a 2026 pilot used improv to train dementia care partners. The boundary: no randomized trial of a full improv course for social anxiety exists; expect exposure-like benefits, not treatment.",
