      "Genealogy is a long-horizon hobby that combines puzzle-solving with identity work. A scoping review of 26 studies found amateur genealogists report changes to personal and family identity and to relationships, and describe the detective work as absorbing; a survey of 775 Australian family historians identified motives of self-understanding, generativity and cognitive challenge. Students who took a semester family-history course showed larger increases in family identification and self-esteem and a larger decrease in anxiety than controls, driven by working with historical records. Nearly two-thirds of hobbyists, however, reported strong distressing emotions at some point. The boundary: one non-randomized course study and self-report surveys; the elite tier reflects the years of effort a real project takes, not the strength of the evidence.",
    action: "Pick one grandparent's line and set a two-year goal of a documented five-generation tree: spend two hours a week in records (census, civil registration, archives), interview the oldest relatives first, and write a short narrative for each ancestor you confirm; decide in advance how you will handle distressing discoveries.",
