    description:
      "A systematic review of the psychological and social benefits of adult sport found wellbeing and reduced distress most often reported, with club- or team-based sport associated with better outcomes than individual activity; the updated review of 29 studies confirmed better mental and social outcomes for adults in team sport and more frequent play. In the Copenhagen City Heart Study, the sports involving most social interaction — tennis, badminton and soccer — were associated with the largest gains in life expectancy (up to 9.7 years for tennis), and Scottish data link sport participation with lower loneliness in older adults. It sits at the elite tier because the benefits accrue over seasons and years of belonging. The boundary: observational studies with substantial selection; injury risk is real.",
